# Score Tool Calls

This notebook scores the tool calls a small order-status agent makes, apart
from the answer it gives. The host runner records every tool call, and two
independent scorers check the trace and the final answer. One run answers
correctly without looking the order up, so its tool score fails while its
answer score passes. The notebook runs offline and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Stub the tool and the agent

`OrderTools` stands in for the order database. `lookup_order` is the only
tool the agent should use to answer a status question.

```elixir
defmodule OrderTools do
  @moduledoc "A stub order lookup with fixed statuses."

  @statuses %{"A100" => "shipped", "A101" => "processing", "A102" => "shipped"}

  def call("lookup_order", %{order_id: id}), do: Map.fetch!(@statuses, id)
end
```

`OrderAgent` stands in for the application's agent. It receives a
`call_tool` function, so it does not know how calls are recorded. It
remembers order `A102` from an earlier conversation and answers that one
without a lookup.

```elixir
defmodule OrderAgent do
  @moduledoc "A stub order-status agent that answers one order question."

  @remembered %{"A102" => "shipped"}

  def answer(%{order_id: id}, call_tool) do
    status =
      case Map.fetch(@remembered, id) do
        {:ok, status} -> status
        :error -> call_tool.("lookup_order", %{order_id: id})
      end

    "Order #{id} is #{status}."
  end
end
```

## Record the trace in the host runner

The host runner gives the agent a `call_tool` function that records each
tool name and its arguments before calling the stub. It returns the answer
and the ordered trace as the run output. The scorers below read only this
output, so they work with any host that records its trace the same way.

```elixir
run_order_agent = fn _agent, request ->
  {:ok, log} = Agent.start_link(fn -> [] end)

  call_tool = fn name, arguments ->
    Agent.update(log, &[%{name: name, arguments: arguments} | &1])
    OrderTools.call(name, arguments)
  end

  answer = OrderAgent.answer(request, call_tool)

  tool_calls =
    log
    |> Agent.get(&Enum.reverse/1)
    |> Enum.with_index(1)
    |> Enum.map(fn {call, step} -> Map.put(call, :step, step) end)

  Agent.stop(log)
  %{answer: answer, tool_calls: tool_calls}
end
```

## Define the scorers

`tool_use` requires the first tool call to be `lookup_order` for the order
in the request, and allows no other calls.

```elixir
tool_use = fn row, %{tool_calls: calls} ->
  expected = [%{step: 1, name: "lookup_order", arguments: %{order_id: row.input.order_id}}]

  if calls == expected do
    %{score: 1, feedback: "looked up #{row.input.order_id}"}
  else
    %{score: 0, feedback: "expected one lookup_order call, got #{inspect(calls)}"}
  end
end
```

`answer` compares the final answer with the expected text and ignores the
trace.

```elixir
answer = fn row, %{answer: text} ->
  if text == row.expected, do: 1, else: 0
end
```

## Evaluate the agent

Use `Tune.new/2` with the host runner, add the requests as test data, and
give each scorer its own name with `Tune.score/4`.

```elixir
requests = [
  %{id: "A100", input: %{order_id: "A100"}, expected: "Order A100 is shipped."},
  %{id: "A101", input: %{order_id: "A101"}, expected: "Order A101 is processing."},
  %{id: "A102", input: %{order_id: "A102"}, expected: "Order A102 is shipped."}
]

{:ok, report} =
  Tune.new(:order_agent, run: run_order_agent)
  |> Tune.data(test: requests)
  |> Tune.score(:tool_use, tool_use)
  |> Tune.score(:answer, answer)
  |> Tune.evaluate()

IO.puts("tool_use mean: #{Float.round(report.summaries.tool_use.mean, 3)}")
IO.puts("answer mean: #{report.summaries.answer.mean}")
```

```text
tool_use mean: 0.667
answer mean: 1.0
```

Every answer is correct, but one run in three skipped the required lookup.

## Inspect the skipped lookup

Find the `A102` observation and print each score with its trace.

```elixir
skipped = Enum.find(report.observations, &(&1.id == "A102"))

IO.puts("tool_use: #{skipped.scores.tool_use} (#{skipped.feedback.tool_use})")
IO.puts("answer: #{skipped.scores.answer}")
IO.puts("answer text: #{skipped.output.answer}")
IO.inspect(skipped.output.tool_calls, label: "tool calls")
```

```text
tool_use: 0 (expected one lookup_order call, got [])
answer: 1
answer text: Order A102 is shipped.
tool calls: []
```

Compare it with a run that looked the order up.

```elixir
looked_up = Enum.find(report.observations, &(&1.id == "A100"))
IO.inspect(looked_up.output.tool_calls, label: "tool calls")
```

```text
tool calls: [%{name: "lookup_order", arguments: %{order_id: "A100"}, step: 1}]
```

The answer score alone would rate all three runs as correct. The separate
tool score shows that `A102` was right by luck of memory, not because the
agent checked the order.

